WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.290
Back to the sorting line.

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.215
This morning, letters don't arrive as letters.

3
00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:12.875
They arrive as a stream of pages, and the pages of one letter run straight into the next.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.891
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:15.891 --> 00:00:21.412
If the client sends one message, with one write, why doesn't the server get it in one read?

6
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.795
Because TCP, the network's way of delivering bytes in order, delivers a stream, not messages.

7
00:00:30.795 --> 00:00:35.422
A read hands you whatever has arrived: half a message, or one and a half.

8
00:00:35.422 --> 00:00:38.275
The cuts fall wherever the network made them.

9
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.062
So the first station needs a way to tell where one letter ends.

10
00:00:43.062 --> 00:00:48.579
The post office has a trick for that: write the page count on the front of every envelope.

11
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.899
That's our letter link.

12
00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:53.879
Each letter has a type number and a body of text.

13
00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:59.534
On the wire it goes behind its length: four bytes of length, one byte of type, then the body.

14
00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:03.244
And the length counts the type byte and the body, not itself.

15
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:06.634
The lesson's CodecDemo encodes two letters.

16
00:01:06.634 --> 00:01:10.906
note-1, type 1, has six bytes of body, so its length is 7.

17
00:01:10.906 --> 00:01:13.558
parcel-1, type 7, has a length of 9.

18
00:01:13.558 --> 00:01:16.357
Together, that's 24 bytes on the wire.

19
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.226
Now let's name the parts.

20
00:01:18.226 --> 00:01:20.881
One whole letter on the wire is a frame.

21
00:01:20.881 --> 00:01:23.934
Finding where frames begin and end is framing.

22
00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:32.828
A station that turns bytes into messages is a decoder, one that turns messages back into bytes is an encoder, and the pair is a codec.

23
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:36.971
In Netty, a decoder extends ByteToMessageDecoder.

24
00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:44.571
It keeps every byte that has arrived so far on one pile, and calls your decode method each time more bytes come in.

25
00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:49.263
Your job: take whole frames off the pile, and leave the rest for later.

26
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.200
Read it as the clerk at the first station.

27
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.632
Fewer than four bytes?

28
00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:56.496
The page count isn't even here yet, so wait.

29
00:01:56.496 --> 00:01:59.295
Then peek at the length, without taking it.

30
00:01:59.295 --> 00:02:00.792
Is the whole body here?

31
00:02:00.792 --> 00:02:02.028
If not, wait again.

32
00:02:02.028 --> 00:02:06.324
Only then skip the four length bytes, and hand on one whole frame.

33
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:14.638
Now the demo feeds those 24 bytes to the server in awkward slices, cut after byte 3, byte 9 and byte 14.

34
00:02:14.638 --> 00:02:21.047
The last station stamps each letter: it sends it back in capitals, with the type plus one.

35
00:02:21.047 --> 00:02:22.827
Which slices get a reply?

36
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.552
Bytes 0 to 3: nothing.

37
00:02:27.552 --> 00:02:32.136
That's three quarters of a page count, which isn't much to go on.

38
00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:34.348
Bytes 3 to 9: nothing again.

39
00:02:34.348 --> 00:02:38.097
The length says 7, and only part of the body is here.

40
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.226
Bytes 9 to 14 complete the first letter, and start the second.

41
00:02:43.226 --> 00:02:45.212
Out comes NOTE-1, type 2.

42
00:02:45.212 --> 00:02:49.264
And 14 to 24 complete the second: PARCEL-1, type 8.

43
00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.695
Here the post office picture breaks.

44
00:02:51.695 --> 00:02:54.790
A real envelope arrives whole, page count and all.

45
00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:59.743
On the wire, even the four-byte length can arrive in two pieces, as it just did.

46
00:02:59.743 --> 00:03:01.909
That's what the first check is for.

47
00:03:02.833 --> 00:03:04.939
One more job for the first station.

48
00:03:04.939 --> 00:03:08.007
What if an envelope claims to hold a million pages?

49
00:03:08.007 --> 00:03:10.533
Or a client sends a length of two billion?

50
00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:17.893
A decoder without a limit would sit on two gigabytes of bytes, waiting patiently for the rest.

51
00:03:17.893 --> 00:03:20.698
So we set a maximum, here 1024 bytes.

52
00:03:20.698 --> 00:03:26.536
In the demo, a length of 5,000 is turned away at once: TooLongFrameException.

53
00:03:26.536 --> 00:03:29.644
Adjusted frame length exceeds 1024: 5004.

54
00:03:29.644 --> 00:03:34.117
That's the 5,000, plus the four bytes of the length itself.

55
00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:37.943
A length you didn't limit is memory a stranger can claim.

56
00:03:37.943 --> 00:03:41.173
The first station is polite, but it isn't gullible.

57
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.427
And you don't have to write this station yourself.

58
00:03:45.427 --> 00:03:49.818
LengthFieldBasedFrameDecoder does the same job, with five numbers.

59
00:03:49.818 --> 00:03:51.748
Frames of at most 1024 bytes.

60
00:03:51.748 --> 00:03:55.541
The length field starts at byte 0, and it's 4 bytes long.

61
00:03:55.541 --> 00:03:56.472
No adjustment.

62
00:03:56.472 --> 00:03:59.932
And strip those 4 bytes before passing the frame on.

63
00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:04.933
So the next station sees just the type and the body, and turns them into a Letter.

64
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:08.211
On the way out, an encoder writes the three fields back.

65
00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:12.683
If you do write your own, four rules from the Javadoc.

66
00:04:12.683 --> 00:04:16.561
Return without taking anything when a frame isn't complete.

67
00:04:16.561 --> 00:04:20.899
Read from readerIndex, where the unread bytes start, never from 0.

68
00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:25.040
Hand frames on as slices, views of the same memory, not copies.

69
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.839
And never mark the decoder @Sharable: it keeps a pile for one connection.

70
00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:37.280
Here's the server's line in code, in Java and in Kotlin: frame first, then decode, then the stamp.

71
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.112
So why doesn't one write arrive as one read?

72
00:04:41.112 --> 00:04:43.560
Because TCP moves pages, not letters.

73
00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:50.508
Frame before you decode: put the length on the envelope, wait for whole frames, and always set a maximum.

74
00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:52.057
Quick check.

75
00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:54.676
Move the cut at byte 9 to byte 11.

76
00:04:54.676 --> 00:04:57.063
Which slice now answers NOTE-1?

77
00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:01.367
Bytes 3 to 11.

78
00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:07.113
The first letter is 11 bytes long, so that slice ends on its last byte.

79
00:05:07.113 --> 00:05:09.299
Then 11 to 14 gets nothing.
